  "query_preview": "..\n     Strong winds and dry conditions leading to potential Critical \nFire Weather...\n\nToday and Friday: A broad ridge is stretching across the Pacific \nNorthwest with the apex centered over Western MT. It will continue \nto  promote the dry, warm pattern that has been in the Inland \nNorthwest since Wednesday. 850mb max temperatures range from 20-21C \nfor Thursday and 22-24C on Friday. It will keep temperatures in the \nupper 80 to 90s for most. Lewiston has around a 50% probability of \nreaching triple digits on Friday. Areas being impacted by wildfire \nsmoke could have a few degrees shaved off the forecast high. \nHeatRisk remains minor to moderate across the region. Afternoon \nwinds will increase with weak afternoon thermal lows will bring \nbrief increases to winds with gusts reaching into the teens. \nOvernight lows will be in the 50s for Thursday and 60s for Friday.\n\nSaturday: This continues to be main focus. Ensembles continue to \nswing a strong +120kt upper level jet across WA as a Low swings into \nthe Lower BC. Any chance of precip across the region will limited to \nthe Cascade Crest. The Low will push a strong dry cold front through \nthe Inland Northwest with 850mb-500mb winds ranging from 25-50kts. \nThe winds will mix down to the surface as the front passes. Westerly \nwinds will begin to increase in the late morning and continue into \nthe evening. Timing for the strongest winds will be in the afternoon \nto early evening hours. Ensembles have been very consistent with \nspeeds and timing. For speeds, sustained winds will be in the 20 to \nlow 30 mph range. Weaker winds in the teens will be over the Lower \nIdaho Panhandle. For gusts, a range of 30 to low 40s for most. The \nLower Idaho Panhandle will have gusts into the 20s. Outside of the \nLower Idaho Panhandle, probability of wind gusts greater than 35 mph \nis at least 60 percent. Southeast Washington, Lewiston, and Western \nColumbia Basin including Waterville Plateau down to Wenatchee has 20-\n40 percent probability for greater than 50 mph gusts. It will be \nsomething to keep an eye on for the next model runs. Highs for the \nday will be in the slightly cooler with a range of upper 70s to low \n90s. Overnight lows will be in the 40s to 50s.\n\nSunday: While temperatures will be a lot cooler, winds will continue \nto be breezy. The region will see winds sustained in the teens with \ngusts into the 20s continuing to impact ongoing and new fires in the \narea. Highs will be in the 70s to mid 80s. Overnight lows will be in \nthe 40s to mid 50s.\n\nMonday through Wednesday: A flat ridge will fill in behinds the \nexiting Low. Temperatures will rebound back into the 80s to low 90s. \nWinds will continue the diurnal trend of breezy afternoons and calm \novernights. The warm, dry pattern will continue with no chances of \nbeneficial precip for the start of August. Overnight lows will be in \nthe 50s. /JDC",
